golang-github-danos-config (1.9danos10) unstable; urgency=medium

  * schema: the reconcile read-back comparison (sameConfigAllowDefaulted)
    now also removes echoed schema defaults inside list entries. 1.9danos9
    walked JSON objects only and stopped at the first array, so a default
    leaf in a list entry (acm rule "action", default "deny") survived in
    the read-back and a component that echoed exactly what it was sent
    still compared unequal: acmd and provisiond stayed "unverified /
    read-back mismatch" after a crash restart (p2p4: 74/81).
    List entries are paired by content, not position (a system-ordered
    list is sorted by each entry's own spelling, which differs between the
    full and the stripped document) and the pruned read-back is normalised
    again before the comparison. A changed, removed or unsent entry still
    fails the comparison.
  * Tests: 6 new cases in component_defaults_test.go.

 -- i-danos dev <dev@i-danos.dev>  Thu, 29 Oct 2026 14:30:00 -0700

golang-github-danos-config (1.9danos9) unstable; urgency=medium

  * schema: reconcile's read-back comparison (sameConfig) now ignores a
    sent subtree when it is only a schema default AND the component
    echoed it back unchanged -- real configuration a component reports
    differently is still compared and still fails as before.
    sameConfigAllowDefaulted replaces the plain byte comparison for
    reconcile specifically; the payload itself is unchanged (1.9danos8's
    attempt to send a defaults-stripped payload made vrrp and kdump
    reject an empty document outright, turning one stuck component into
    two failures -- reverted here, replaced by this approach).
  * Tests: 6 new cases in component_defaults_test.go (empty read-back vs
    an all-default payload, echoed defaults, a default overridden by real
    configuration, non-default content still compared, plain equality
    with no defaults, extra read-back content never sent).

 -- i-danos dev <dev@i-danos.dev>  Wed, 28 Oct 2026 04:30:00 -0700

golang-github-danos-config (1.9danos8) unstable; urgency=medium

  * union: reconcile's FilterSetTree target no longer sends components
    their own YANG-default leaves. A union.Node always materialises
    defaults (needed so "show configuration all" can display them), but
    reconcile's comparison against a component's own read-back used that
    same raw node, so a component that was never configured still got a
    payload with its defaults filled in while its read-back reported {}.
    sameConfig never matched, and the component stayed permanently
    "unverified" in the commit journal (R14, seen on vrrp after a P2/P4
    crash restart: "dirty": {"net.vyatta.vci.vrrp.v1": {"reason":
    "read-back mismatch"}}). NewDataNodeWithoutDefaults wraps a union.Node
    to recursively omit default-only values (at any depth) while still
    exposing anything actually configured; only the reconcile target uses
    it, so "show configuration" and friends are unaffected.
  * Tests: 3 new union tests (fixture leaks defaults as a sanity check;
    wrapper omits them; wrapper keeps explicitly configured leaves;
    wrapper recurses into all-default non-presence containers).

 -- i-danos dev <dev@i-danos.dev>  Wed, 28 Oct 2026 00:00:00 -0700

golang-github-danos-config (1.9danos7) unstable; urgency=medium

  * auth/acmd: an rpc or notification rule without rpc-name /
    notification-name (YANG: such a rule matches everything) is no longer read
    back as "rpc-name":null / "notification-name":null. The null made
    configd's read-back comparison fail, so reconcile reported "Not
    reconciled: acmd (read-back mismatch)" and the component stayed dirty
    (found on a real VM after a P1 crash restart). Same cause and fix as the
    action/operation leaves in 1.9danos6: omitempty on both fields.
  * Tests: getter round-trip cases for rules without a name.

 -- i-danos dev <dev@i-danos.dev>  Sat, 24 Oct 2026 09:00:00 -0700

golang-github-danos-config (1.9danos6) unstable; urgency=medium

  * schema: component compensation on ordinary commit failure (A02 phase 3).
    The failing component is compensated first, then the components applied
    in the same commit in reverse order, with the previous configuration as
    baseline; every step is reported. Calls to components are bounded (a
    timeout only stops waiting, the call cannot be recalled) and an
    unanswered Check fails the commit after 30 s with nothing applied.
    Dispatches are recorded through a recorder interface so configd can keep
    a crash-safe journal; unknown outcomes are verified by reading the
    component back and comparing with sameConfig.
  * schema: sameConfig normalises through the YANG schema and compares
    lists and leaf-lists order-independently except "ordered-by user" ones,
    whose order is part of the configuration; duplicates are not collapsed.
  * auth/acmd: the getter returns exactly what configd delivered. Presence
    containers stay {} and never-sent leaves (the *-default actions, rule
    action/operation, group and module names) are omitted instead of being
    emitted as empty strings, which made read-back verification fail.
  * Tests: compensation, bounded Check, sameConfig (including a run against
    the real acm YANG when DANOS_YANG_DIR is set; skipped otherwise), and a
    getter round-trip over 15 payloads.
  * Version note: danos4 and danos5 were intermediate local builds and were
    never published.

 -- i-danos dev <dev@i-danos.dev>  Fri, 23 Oct 2026 12:00:00 -0700

golang-github-danos-config (1.9danos3) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Correct danos2: it only found and fixed schema/component_mgr.go's use of
    github.com/godbus/dbus, missing auth/acmd/policy-reload.go's own,
    separate use of the fork's Handler/SignalHandler extensions (a
    recursive grep across the tree, not just one level deep, finds both).
    Worse, danos2's fix was a real correctness bug, not just incomplete:
    component_mgr.go's `err.(dbus.Error)` assertion checks errors returned
    by cm.SetConfigForModel, whose real implementation is vci's own
    dbusTransport (vendored, using the fork's dbus.Conn) -- so switching
    just that one file to real v5's dbus.Error made the type assertion
    permanently fail at runtime (compiles fine, silently never matches).
  * Fix instead mirrors vci's own: vendor github.com/danos/godbus/dbus and
    github.com/danos/objtree under vendor/ (copied directly from vci's
    vendor tree, same private import paths, same patches already applied),
    and repoint both policy-reload.go and component_mgr.go at the vendored
    copies -- restoring the single consistent dbus type this package always
    had before, rather than splitting it across two incompatible ones.
    Drops golang-dbus-dev and golang-github-godbus-dbus-dev from
    debian/control entirely; the real v5 package is still pulled in
    transitively via golang-github-danos-vci-dev's own
    golang-go-systemd-dev dependency, satisfying whatever needed it, but
    this package no longer needs to reference either dbus package itself.
    Verified: `go build ./...` and `go test ./...` both pass locally.

 -- DANOS <noreply@danos.io>  Mon, 28 Sep 2026 11:30:00 +0800

golang-github-danos-config (1.9danos2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * schema/component_mgr.go: switch to Debian trixie's real golang-dbus-dev
    (5.1.0) instead of this project's v4-era golang-github-godbus-dbus-dev
    fork. The import path itself stays the plain "github.com/godbus/dbus":
    confirmed by dpkg -L that Debian's package installs its v5 source under
    that same unversioned GOPATH path, not github.com/godbus/dbus/v5 (a
    packaging convention, not something upstream's own go.mod declares).
    This package's only use of the package is a plain dbus.Error type
    assertion for error handling -- not any of the fork's
    Handler/SignalHandler extensions -- and that type is identical in real
    upstream v5, confirmed by a local build and full test pass against the
    real golang-dbus-dev package.
  * This was blocking the OBS build outright, the same way it blocked vci
    before vci's own fix: this package's build root needed both the v4 fork
    (for its own direct import) and Debian's real v5 (transitively, via
    golang-github-danos-vci-dev's own golang-go-systemd-dev dependency),
    which cannot coexist -- "dpkg: trying to overwrite '.../godbus/dbus/
    auth.go', which is also in package golang-dbus-dev (5.1.0-1)". Here the
    fix is simpler than vci's vendoring: since this package's own usage
    never needed the fork's extensions, moving to the real v5 outright
    removes the conflict instead of working around it.

 -- DANOS <noreply@danos.io>  Mon, 28 Sep 2026 11:15:00 +0800

